g up in north london  i started rapping as soon as i  discovered my brother's  hidden uh stash of hip-hop cassettes  that he told me explicitly not to listen  to because they had swearing on them  so as soon as he left the house as a 10  year old i was there just memorizing  wu-tang clan lyrics i always felt that  there was this volcano inside of me  bursting to get out  when did you first start acting i think  in a way i've been acting since i was a  young kid  i came from a working class pakistani  household but then i got scholarships to  go to  predominantly white upper class private  schools  so i kind of in a way feel like i was  changing costume changing character  every day growing up but it was really  some school teachers that realized that  okay  this kid's got a bit too much  hyperactive energy we need to do  something with it  it was really something that anchored me  through teenage years that were  you know somewhat troubled but ahmed  never imagined he could make a career  of performing i'm curious if there was  one particular direction you thought you  might be headed in  yeah i thought i was heading the  direction of not being able to do any of  it  and it was interesting in a way you know  to kind of enter  um a profession with no expectations  can actually be very liberating because  you dance like no one's watching no one  is watching  ahmed was in a string of indie films in  the uk  but had yet to make it big i was  thinking of giving up acting at that  point  because i'd really hit a wall in my  career then came  a pivotal audition to co-star with jake  gyllenhaal  in the 2014 thriller nightcrawler  initially were you told you weren't  right for that part yeah very much so  making peace with what you don't have  that's what it's all about that again  kind of took a certain pressure off i  think i kind of thrive  in situations where people don't see me  coming yeah you know i kind of probably  enjoy being the underdog  he got a call back they said in order to  get to the next round you have to come  to la  but we can't fly you out there so pretty  much with what i had left in my bank  account i booked that flight and and  thought i'd just  take a chance the breakout role launched  ahmed's career in the u.s  finally opening doors to high-profile  films and franchises like star wars  rogue one and marvel's venom  and he was cast as the protagonist in  the gripping  hbo drama the night of riz  ahmed that performance won him the lead  actor emmy  in 2017. he was the first asian and  first muslim  to win the award if this show has shown  a light  on some of the prejudice in our society  islamophobia  some of the uh injustice in our justice  system then  maybe that's something and while ahmed  could make history  at this year's academy awards he says  the real win  is seeing new kinds of stories on screen  we all know what it is to feel like an  outsider and so for myself with this  character and with this project it was  really fascinating to play someone who  falls between the cracks of hearing  culture and deaf culture  and what he realized is the same thing  that i realized and i hope audiences do  which is that  deafness actually isn't a disability for  many people it's it's a culture  have you given any thought to a speech  an acceptance speech no i  i haven't to be honest this is a film  that was made on a shoestring budget  with just  a lot of heart a lot of dedication a lot  of blood sweat and tears you know  literally  and to see it get that recognition  that's everything well you certainly put  your heart into it  i did you know i actually said to darius  after we shot the film  you know if no one sees this it's okay  i've gotten so much from this experience  already  so um that doesn't mean i don't want  people to see it i'd actually love it if  people saw the film  check it out yeah and you should see it  he's unbelievable in this movie it's a  small film but he makes it big  uh he's an incredible actor and again  one of the things that really struck me  in that conversation when i asked him  when did you start acting  and he said in a way i've always been  acting and it's you know he's had to in  effect across cultures growing up  in england listen i saw him in the night  the night of on hbo  he was knockout in that he was that was  such a great series and he was so good  but i like this anthony where he says i  thrive in places where people don't see  me coming  i always think it's always good to be  underestimated and then over delivered  it seems like he's done that time and  time again  yeah i'm glad you mentioned the night up  too because that's a sensational series  that i think a lot of people missed all  right and you can hear more of my  conversation with riz ahmed  on today's cbs this morning podcast
 

